<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Szlachta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The szlachta was the hereditary noble class of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Unusually numerous, it enjoyed extensive privileges, including the right to elect the king and to participate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>. Accounts of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth describe the szlachta as the political nation of the state, whose liberties shaped its distinctive system of noble democracy but also contributed to governmental paralysis.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origins of the szlachta are obscure, and its members themselves called it perennial. Two theories were popular among its members and early historians: descent from the ancient Iranian Sarmatians, and descent from Japheth, a son of Noah, while the peasantry were said to descend from Ham. The szlachta secured increasing power from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a>, who ruled from 1333 to 1370, until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> in the late 18th century. In 1413 the Lithuanian and Ruthenian nobilities formally joined the Polish nobility, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-horodlo">Union of Horodło</a> forty-seven families of Catholic Lithuanian lords and boyars were adopted by Polish noble families.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The szlachta made up about 8 to 10 per cent of Polish-Lithuanian society, against about 1 per cent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and under 1 per cent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Their land was held in allodium, not as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, and they stood as equals before the king, which Adam Zamoyski argues set them apart from the feudal nobility of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. Their rights included voting for kings, freedom from arbitrary arrest and from corporal punishment, and a monopoly on landownership. The lower chamber of parliament, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>, was composed of deputies elected at local sejmiks.</p><p>The extinction of the male lines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piasts</a> and then the Jagiellons let the nobles choose kings from the dynasties' female descendants, and each king on election granted privileges in his Pacta conventa, the basis of the Golden Liberty. By wealth the nobility divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, middle nobility and petty nobility, yet few legal distinctions separated them. In the 17th and 18th centuries the prevailing ideology was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmatism">Sarmatism</a>, which shaped dress, such as the żupan and kontusz, and made the sabre nearly obligatory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ennoblement was rare: about 800 lawful grants are estimated between the 14th century and the mid-18th century, and from 1641 only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> could make them. During the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partitions-of-poland">Partitions of Poland</a> between 1772 and 1795 most nobles began to lose their legal privileges and social status, and their legal status then depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy until 1918. The estate was officially abolished in 1921 by the March Constitution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Szlachta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was an elective monarchy in which the king was chosen by the entire <b>szlachta</b> nobility in mass elections, usually held at Wola outside Warsaw.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Great lords limited kings, as the English barons did through Magna Carta in 1215, and in some states nobles ruled outright: the patricians of the Republic of Venice, or the <b>szlachta</b> of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth, who from 1573 elected their kings.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
